    89	## 0. Notation and setting
    90	
    91	t := log N; E := t^{1/3}(log t)^{2/3}; Q := t^{2/3}(log t)^{1/3}; D′ := Q/E = (t/log t)^{1/3}. So E = D′ log t,
    92	Q = D′² log t, D′Q = t, and L_N[1/3, c] = e^{(c+o(1))E}, L_N[2/3, c] = e^{(c+o(1))Q}.
    93	
    94	The architecture is that of Theorem A (`thmA_proof.md` §3) with recursion (recurse §1, §3.1): rows r² ≡ R, a Gauss
    95	split R ≡ u₀/v₀, complete factorisation of |u₀|, |v₀| by a recursive call, descent of every large prime factor by
    96	degree-one special-q relations in one field A = (g₀, g₁, w, c) with rational side G = g₁x − g₀y and algebraic side
    97	F_A of degree d, LV6 linear algebra, fruitfulness by Theorem DD. The size model of recurse §3.4 (d = δD′, factor
    98	base B = e^{bE}, box inflation e^{sE}, children ≤ e^{λz} for a target of size e^z, O(log t) levels) is the target.
    99	
   100	A *target* is a rational prime q (lattice {P : q | G(P)}) or a degree-one prime ideal 𝔮 = (q, r) of K = Q(α)
   101	(lattice {(a, b) : a ≡ rb mod q}). The *children* of a box point P for a target are the rational primes > B of the
   102	rational cofactor and the degree-one prime ideals > B of the algebraic cofactor. Rational children are integers;
   103	algebraic children are ideals of K.
   104	
   105	## 1. What dBPW prove (read in the original, arXiv 2512.01588v2, 19 Feb 2026)
   106	
   107	**Setting and hypotheses.**
   108	- K any number field, degree n ≥ 2 (no upper bound), discriminant Δ_K, residue ρ_K of ζ_K at 1.
   109	- Input: an LLL-reduced basis of O_K (Thms 1.1, 9.5, 13.2). §9.5 (Lemma 9.14, Remark 9.15) gives a variant for an
   110	  order R ⊂ O_K with the conductor in the modulus and radius |Δ(R)|^{3/(2n)}, |Δ(R)| = [O_K : R]²|Δ_K| (found by the
   111	  codex blind round; I checked that §9.5 exists but did not audit it). So computing O_K is not an unavoidable
   112	  obstruction; the discriminant scale only gets worse.
   113	- ERH = the Riemann hypothesis for Hecke L-functions of K (§2.2). It enters through the random-walk theorem
   114	  (Thm 6.4 / Cor 6.5: walk on Pic⁰_{K𝔪} with N ≈ 7n + 2log(1/ε) + log|Pic⁰| steps by uniform prime ideals of norm
   115	  ≤ B = Õ(n²(n² (log log 1/ε)² + (log |Δ_K|N𝔪)²))), through prime-ideal sampling (Lemma 5.4), and through
   116	  smooth-ideal density estimates in Part 2. Theorem 7.1 (the averaging statement) is unconditional.
   117	- **Density notion (Def 2.11).** δ_𝒮[x] = min_{t ∈ [x/e^n, x]} |{𝔟 ∈ 𝒮 : N𝔟 ≤ t}|/(ρ_K t): the proportion of 𝒮
   118	  among *all integral ideals* of norm ≤ t, for t in a window of ratio e^n below x. It is not a relative density
   119	  among primes, nor among values of any polynomial.
   120	
   121	**Theorem 7.1 (unconditional).** If the Arakelov ray class of the input is uniform on a coset of G ≤ Pic⁰_{K𝔪} and
   122	ρ ≥ 8n²Γ_K|Δ_K|^{1/(2n)}N(𝔪)^{1/n} (Γ_K ≤ |Δ_K|^{1/n}), then for α uniform in Exp(𝔞) ∩ ρB_∞,
   123	E Pr[(α)Exp(−𝔞) ∈ 𝒮 | ray condition] ≥ (N𝔪₀/φ(𝔪₀))·[Pic⁰:G]·δ_𝒮[ρ^n]/3.
   124	
   125	**Theorem 9.5 (ERH).** For *every* input ideal 𝔟 (worst case) and any family 𝒮 (with the class condition
   126	[d⁰(𝒮)] ⊆ G − [d⁰(𝔟)]), Algorithm 2 outputs β ∈ 𝔟 with (β)𝔟^{−1} ∈ 𝒮·𝒮_B^G, β ∈ τK_{𝔪,1},
