   129	…). The success event is (β)𝔟^{−1} = 𝔠·∏_{i ≤ N}𝔭_i with known walk primes 𝔭_i and N𝔠 ≤ r^n.
   130	Thm 1.1 is the specialisation G = Pic⁰, 𝔪 = O_K; Thm 13.2 the version with success ≥ δ_𝒮[r^n]/6.
   131	
   132	**§14.** A purely combinatorial lower bound δ_{𝒮_{A,B}}[x] ≥ (4 log B)^{1−u}u^{−u}/(ρ_K B) for ideals composed of
   133	primes with norm in (A, B] (counting products, as in LP92 Thm 6.1). Codex nested_1 gives the analogous bound for the
   134	monoid of an *arbitrary* set of m ≥ δy/log y prime ideals of norm ≤ y: δ ≥ (δ/(u log y))^u/(ρ_K y).
   135	
   136	**Theorem 17.7 (ERH).** If S contains all prime ideals of norm ≤ B_max = poly(L_{|Δ|}(1/2), L_{n^n}(2/3),
   137	ρ_K^{cut}), Algorithm 3 writes any 𝔞 coprime to 𝔪₀ as αO_K·∏_{𝔭∈S}𝔭^{−v_𝔭} in expected time
   138	poly(L_{|Δ|}(1/2), L_{n^n}(2/3), size, ρ_K^{cut}, …). One sampling step, repeated until the cofactor is smooth.
   139	
   140	**Answers to the task's questions.**
   141	- *Degree range:* unrestricted. *Density:* among all ideals of norm ≈ r^n (local density, Def 2.11).
   142	- *One-sided vs two-sided:* strictly one-sided. The output β ∈ 𝔞 is a general element of a rank-n lattice. The NFS
   143	  set {a − bα} is a rank-2 subgroup of K, not an ideal (an ideal contains cO_K for some c ≠ 0, so has rank n), so it
   144	  cannot be an input; conditioning dBPW's output on lying in it destroys the bound (codex: O(H²) of Θ(H^n) points).
   145	- *Rational side:* none. ψ(β) = Σβ_i m^i mod N has no small rational counterpart for a general β.
   146	- *Level structure:* one step. The cofactor bound N𝔠 ≤ r^n does not shrink with N𝔞 (the N(𝔞) cancels), so iterating
   147	  gives no contraction.
   148	- *Cofactor scale for NFS fields.* For a degree-n polynomial with log‖f‖ = O(Q) (the size-model fields),
   149	  log|disc f| = O(n log‖f‖ + n log n) = O(t) (Hadamard on the Sylvester matrix), so log|Δ_K| = O(t). The typical
   150	  value is log|Δ_K| ≍ t (the polynomial discriminant is ≍ ‖f‖^{2n−2}); we do not prove a lower bound for the field
   151	  discriminant of the random fields. dBPW's certified cofactor scale is then N^{3ζ/2+o(1)} when log|Δ_K| = ζt
   152	  (codex (14), with b = 2 the extra terms are o(t) at n ≍ D′).
   153	
   154	## 2. Theorem AF: the cofactor law of Arakelov-uniform sampling
   155	
   156	**Theorem AF [proved; it is dBPW's computation (36)–(48) read as a two-sided identity].** Let K have degree n ≥ 3,
   157	r* := r₁ + r₂ − 1 ≥ 1, trivial modulus. Let the Arakelov class of 𝔞 ∈ Div⁰_K be uniform on Pic⁰_K, let
   158	ρ ≥ 8n²Γ_K|Δ_K|^{1/(2n)}, and let α be uniform in Exp(𝔞) ∩ ρB_∞. For any set 𝒮 of integral ideals put
   159	δ̃_𝒮(x) := |𝒮(x)|/(ρ_K x), X := ρ^n. Then
   160	
   161	  e^{−1/4}·𝔼_v[δ̃_𝒮(Xe^{−v})·1_{v ≤ log X}] ≤ Pr[(α)·Exp(−𝔞) ∈ 𝒮] ≤ e^{1/4}·𝔼_v[δ̃_𝒮(Xe^{−v})·1_{v ≤ log X}],
   162	
   163	with v ~ Gamma(r*, 1) (density v^{r*−1}e^{−v}/(r*−1)!).
   164	
   165	*Proof.* For a single ideal 𝔠, p_{𝔞,𝔠} := Pr_α[(α)Exp(−𝔞) = 𝔠] equals |Exp(𝔞 + d(𝔠))^× ∩ ρB_∞| divided by
   166	|Exp(𝔞) ∩ ρB_∞| (dBPW Lemma 7.4 with trivial modulus; Exp(·)^× is the set of generators). The denominator lies in
   167	[e^{−1/4}, e^{1/4}]·2^{r₁}(2π)^{r₂}ρ^n/√|Δ_K| for every 𝔞 (dBPW (34), from Lemma 2.8, using the hypothesis on ρ).
   168	The numerator depends only on the Arakelov class, and its expectation over a uniform class is exactly
   169	w·Vol(S_{n log ρ − log N𝔠})/(h_K·Vol(T)) = w(n log ρ − log N𝔠)_+^{r*}/(r*!·h_K R_K) (dBPW (38)–(42), Lemma 2.4:
   170	the expected number of points of a uniformly shifted log-unit lattice in a simplex is volume over covolume; these are
   171	equalities). With the class number formula ρ_K = 2^{r₁}(2π)^{r₂}h_K R_K/(w√|Δ_K|) this gives
   172	E_𝔞 p_{𝔞,𝔠} ∈ [e^{−1/4}, e^{1/4}]·C(N𝔠)/(ρ_K X), C(N) := (log(X/N))_+^{r*}/r*! (dBPW (44), (46)). Summing over
   173	𝔠 ∈ 𝒮 and applying Abel summation as in dBPW (47):
   174	Σ_{𝔠∈𝒮}C(N𝔠) = ∫_1^X |𝒮(t)|(log(X/t))^{r*−1}/((r*−1)!·t) dt; the substitution t = Xe^{−v} turns
   175	Σ_{𝔠∈𝒮} C(N𝔠)/(ρ_K X) into 𝔼_v[δ̃_𝒮(Xe^{−v})1_{v≤log X}]. ∎
   176	
   177	**Remarks.**
   178	- dBPW use only the lower half (with δ_𝒮[X] ≤ δ̃_𝒮(t) for t ∈ [X/e^n, X], and Pr[v ≤ n] ≥ 1/2). The upper half shows
   179	  their bound is tight up to e^{±1/4} and the Gamma weighting: *the method succeeds exactly as often as a random ideal
   180	  of norm Xe^{−v} lies in 𝒮.* No cleverness in choosing 𝒮 or ρ changes the norm scale X ≥ (8n²)^n√|Δ_K|.
   181	- For 𝒮 = {N𝔠 ≤ T}: Pr[N(cofactor) ≤ T] ≤ e^{1/4}(n log ρ)^{r*}·|{𝔠 : N𝔠 ≤ T}|/(r*!·ρ_K·ρ^n). Equivalently the
   182	  log-norm of the cofactor is n log ρ − v up to the weight δ̃: cofactor norms below X·e^{−V} carry Gamma tail
   183	  weight e^{−V}Σ_{j<r*}V^j/j! (`barrier.af_weight`).
   184	- The theorem is about the averaged (Haar) step; dBPW's ERH random walk makes the actual input ε-close to it, so the
   185	  same identity holds for Algorithm 2 up to ±ε.
   186	
   187	**Corollary AF [heuristic in its quantitative step].** Let log|Δ_K| ≥ ζt. Every Arakelov-type step (Thm 7.1/9.5 or
   188	any sampler whose input class is near-uniform) succeeds for a family 𝒮 with probability ≈ the local density of 𝒮 at
   189	norm Xe^{−v}, X ≥ |Δ_K|^{1/2}. Moving the effective norm down by e^{−V} costs Gamma weight ≈ e^{−V}V^{r*}/r*!, while
   190	for 𝒮 = 𝒮_y (y-smooth ideals) the density gains only a factor ≈ e^{V log u/log y} with log u/log y = o(1); so the
   191	weighted average is governed by norms X·e^{−O(n log n)} = N^{ζ/2+o(1)} (n ≤ t^{1/3+o(1)}). If smooth ideals of norm X have density
   192	≤ u^{−(1+o(1))u} (u = log X/log y), which is the standard heuristic and the value dBPW's own analysis uses, then
   193	making such a cofactor e^{bΛ_α}-smooth costs exp((ζ(1−α)/(2b) + o(1))t^{1−α}(log t)^α) = L[1 − α]. Balancing
   194	against a factor base L[α] forces α ≥ 1/2, and iterating the step gives no contraction (the cofactor scale does not
   195	depend on the input norm). This is exactly dBPW's L_{|Δ|}(1/2) (codex nested_1 §3(c) independently).
   196	
   197	**Remark (one-sided rows; codex nested_1 (21)–(22)).** With the evaluation map ψ: R → Z/N of kernel 𝔫 (N𝔫 = N), the
   198	ray condition β ≡ R = r² (mod 𝔫) gives valid one-sided rows r² = ψ(β), with no rational side needed. But the modulus
   199	puts N into the radius: r^n ⊇ |Δ|^{3/2}·N. This is "Dixon's method in K", at norm ≥ N√|Δ|: L[1/2] at best.
   200	
   201	## 3. Prop SI: the sparse-image dilemma
   202	
   203	**Proposition SI [proved].** Fix a node: a finite candidate set Ω (the box), a field A, and the value map
   204	Φ_A: Ω → 𝓘_X (ideals, or integers, of norm ≤ X; |Ω| ≤ W). Let 𝒮(A) ⊆ 𝓘_X be any "good family" allowed to depend
   205	on A.
   206	- (i) *Fixed field.* If 𝒮(A) := 𝓘_X ∖ Φ_A(Ω), then Pr_{P∈Ω}[Φ_A(P) ∈ 𝒮(A)] = 0, while the relative density of
   207	  𝒮(A) is ≥ 1 − W/|𝓘_X|, and its local density satisfies δ_{𝒮(A)}[x] ≥ δ_{all}[x] − e^nW/(ρ_K x) (codex (19)).
   208	  Hence no lemma of the form "for every family of relative density ≥ η the node hits it with probability ≥ f(η) > 0"
   209	  holds for a fixed field when W = o(|𝓘_X|).
   210	- (ii) *Averaging over the field.* If 𝒮 does not depend on A and, for each P, the law of Φ_A(P) over A has density
   211	  ≥ c with respect to the uniform law on some set 𝓘′ (a full-support sampler on 𝓘′), then
   212	  E_A Pr_P[Φ_A(P) ∈ 𝒮] ≥ c·|𝒮 ∩ 𝓘′|/|𝓘′|.
   213	- (iii) In nested descent the good family of a child is "descendable in the field the child lives in". For an
   214	  algebraic child that field is A itself, so (ii) does not apply and (i) shows that density alone cannot suffice.
   215	
   216	*Proof.* (i): Φ_A(Ω) has at most W elements; delete them from every cumulative count. (ii): linearity. (iii): an
   217	algebraic child is an ideal of K_A (Lemma FB below). ∎
   218	
   219	**The principle behind every existing arbitrary-family tool.** Each is counting plus a sampler that is uniform on a
   220	group; the group's size fixes the value size.
   221	- Theorem K / Lemma S″ (recurse §3.2; codex (26)): R uniform on (Z/N)^{×2}; every coprime same-class pair (u, v) is hit
   222	  with probability 2^ω/φ(N); value size |u₀v₀| ≈ N.
   223	- LP92 Thm 6.1 (p. 499, read in the original): a pure count of integers ≤ x composed of primes from an arbitrary set
   224	  𝒫, under y ≤ exp((log x)^{1/2}(log log x)^η) and two mass conditions on 𝒫. LP use it with forms uniform over a class
   225	  group (§§5, 10), at value size ≈ √|Δ|. Its y-range already excludes x = e^{Θ(t)}, y = e^{Θ(Q)} (codex).
   226	- dBPW: Arakelov-uniform input, value size ≥ √|Δ_K| (Theorem AF).
   227	In an NFS node the candidate set has W = e^{O(E)} points while the values have size X = e^{Θ(z d)} with z ≥ bE, so
   228	W/X → 0 and only route (ii) is available.
   229	
   230	**Class obstruction (codex (18)).** If the good primes all lie in a proper subgroup H of Cl(K) and [𝔮] ∉ H, no relation
   231	(β) = 𝔮∏𝔭^{e} exists. Relative prime density cannot see this; including all small primes removes this particular
   232	obstruction but proves nothing two-sided.
   233	
   234	## 4. Fresh fields: what can and cannot be made exogenous
   235	
   236	**Lemma FB (algebraic children are field-bound) [proved].** Let K₁ ⊂ K₂ with [K₂ : K₁] = e ≥ 2, K₂ = K₁(γ), and let
   237	𝔮 ⊂ O_{K₁} be a prime of residue field F_q such that γ − c ∉ 𝔮O_{K₂} for every c ∈ Z. If a, b ∈ Z and
   238	a − bγ ∈ 𝔮O_{K₂}, then q | a and q | b. *Proof.* O_{K₂}/𝔮O_{K₂} is an F_q-algebra in which 1 and γ̄ are F_q-linearly
   239	independent (otherwise γ ≡ c mod 𝔮O_{K₂} for some c ∈ Z), so ā − b̄γ̄ = 0 forces ā = b̄ = 0. ∎ So degree-one
   240	elements of an extension cannot carry the whole extended ideal 𝔮O_{K₂}; they can contain one prime 𝔔 | 𝔮, which
   241	leaves the other primes above 𝔮 uncancelled (codex (23): passing to the norm q loses the conjugates). In unrelated
   242	fields the ideal 𝔮 of K₁ has no meaning at all. Algebraic children must therefore be descended in their own field.
   243	(The hypothesis on γ fails only for 𝔮 dividing a fixed index-type quantity; it is not needed for the conclusion that
   244	the ideal is field-bound, only for the tower variant.)
   245	
   246	**Prop R (rational children can be made exogenous) [proved, structural].** A rational child is an integer. A relation
   247	in field A_j (rational side G_j(P)/g₁^{(j)} ≡ ψ_j(a − bα_j) mod N) and one in an unrelated field A_{j+1} can be
   248	multiplied, and the final linear algebra has one block of algebraic columns per field and shared rational columns
   249	(Coppersmith-type multiple-field bookkeeping; each field needs its own LV6 square root). So a design in which level
   250	j + 1 uses a field independent of levels ≤ j sees its rational targets as exogenous: conditional on the history, the
   251	target q is fixed and the field A_{j+1} is fresh, so route (ii) of Prop SI applies to q. Two caveats (codex
   252	nested_1 §3(d)): all targets at one level share A_{j+1}, so a *joint* statement over the m targets of a node is still
   253	needed; and the bound must hold conditionally on the history that selected q.
   254	
   255	**Prop R2 (the price: floor 2/5) [size-model arithmetic].** Suppose no node may have algebraic children, so every
   256	algebraic value must be B-smooth, B = L[α]. A level-1 target ℓ ≥ L[1 − α] (forced by the split, recurse §3.3 (R1))
   257	has algebraic value |F(P)| ≥ ‖f‖·V^d with ‖f‖ ≥ N^{1/(d+1)} (a polynomial with a root mod N) and V ≥ √ℓ (lattice of
   258	index ℓ), so log|F| ≥ t/(d + 1) + (d/2)log ℓ ≥ (√2 − o(1))·√(t·log ℓ) ≥ (√2 − o(1)) t^{1−α/2}(log t)^{α/2}.
   259	B-smoothness at cost L[α] needs log|F|·log u/log B = O(t^α(log t)^{1−α}), i.e. 1 − α/2 ≤ 2α, i.e. **α ≥ 2/5**.
   260	That is the one-level floor; deeper rational-only levels cannot lower it. So exogenous (fresh-field) nesting is
   261	possible exactly where it is not needed. (The lower bound |F(P)| ≥ ‖f‖V^d/polylog holds for all but a small
   262	fraction of box points by the area of {|F| ≤ X}; we use it only as size-model input.)
   263	
   264	## 5. Prop OD: overdetermination of same-field configurations
   265	
   266	**Lemma OD [proved].** Fix (g₀, g₁, w), so F_A(P) = F̂(P) + G(P)·⟨c, v(P)⟩ with v(P) = (a^{d−1}, a^{d−2}b, …, b^{d−1})
   267	and c ∈ Z^d. For s > d points P₁, …, P_s with G(P_i) ≠ 0 there are integers λ_i, not all 0, such that
   268	Σ_i λ_i F_A(P_i)/G(P_i) = Σ_i λ_i F̂(P_i)/G(P_i) for every c. Over the full law (g, w, c) the vector
   269	(F_A(P_i))_{i≤s} ranges over the integer points of an image of a (d + 3)-dimensional family, so for s > d + 3 its
   270	law is supported on a variety of positive codimension. *Proof.* s vectors in Q^d are linearly dependent; the second
   271	statement is a dimension count. ∎
   272	
   273	**Children count [size model].** A target of size e^z has |F(P)| ≥ e^{(d/2)z}·‖f‖ (as in Prop R2). If children are
   274	≤ e^{λz}, the node has m ≥ (d/2)/λ algebraic children (`barrier.min_children`). Hence:
   275	- below 2/5 (exponent contraction, λ → 0 in log terms) m/d → ∞: already one node with its children has more
   276	  values than the field has parameters;
   277	- at α = 1/3 with fixed λ ∈ (1/2, 1) a node and its children (≈ d/(2λ) + 1 values) fit, but a pair of such
   278	  configurations (needed for a second moment) does not, and depth 2 (node, children, grandchildren) does not
   279	  (`barrier.overdetermined`).
   280	
   281	**Consequence (method barrier).** FM, Kern and LD* work by fixing the points and using that the values are
   282	independent-ish arithmetic progressions in c. For a depth-≥ 2 descent (below 2/5), or for the second moment of a
   283	depth-1 configuration (at 1/3), the values are affinely dependent in c; a proof would need an *L-scale smooth
   284	correlation estimate for an overdetermined system of affine forms with huge coefficients*. The literature has
   285	nothing of the kind: Harper (Compositio, arXiv 1408.1662) handles a + b = c for y ≥ (log x)^K; Matthiesen–Wang (ANT
   286	2025) need Ψ(N, y) > N/log⁸N; Lachand needs fixed u; BBDT's binary-form results need y ≥ x^{d−2+ε} (x^{1/√e+ε}
   287	for cubics). This is a barrier to the method, not an impossibility theorem: one random object can support many
   288	correlated searches (codex).
   289	
   290	**Information version [proved, trivial].** After d successful nodes with independent v(P_i) and their values revealed,
   291	c is determined. Any later node's success is a deterministic function of (A, target). So beyond depth 1 every needed
   292	statement is pointwise in the field, i.e. about smooth values of one fixed binary form in special-q lattices at
   293	L-scale; even primes in such values are known only for special forms (Heath-Brown x³ + 2y³; Maynard's incomplete
   294	norm forms need ≥ 7n/8-type variable counts).
   295	
   296	## 6. Precision: backtracking and union bounds
   297	
   298	**Prop BT [model computation].** In a descent whose good sets have density p_j at level j, a node with m children
   299	must retry until all children succeed; with independent children the expected work multiplies by
   300	m·p_{j+1}^{−m} per level. Over O(log t) levels with m ≍ D′ (α = 1/3):
   301	log(overhead) = Σ_j (log m + m log(1/p_j)).
   302	- p_j ≥ (log N)^{−C}: log(overhead) ≍ D′·(log t)² = E·log t, so the cost is exp(O(E log log N)) =
   303	  L[1/3]^{O(log log N)}, i.e. exp((log N)^{1/3}(log log N)^{5/3+o(1)}), not L[1/3, c] (`barrier.backtrack_log_cost`;
   304	  codex (b): a t^{−c} loss per level becomes e^{−O(log² t)}, and raised to 2k it costs e^{O(E log t)}).
   305	- p_j ≥ c₀ > 0 on average: log(overhead) = O(D′ log t) = O(E), so L[1/3, O(1)].
   306	So the nested lemma requested in the task ("relative density ≥ (log N)^{−C}") is not sufficient for L[1/3, c] by
   307	itself; it would give L[1/3 + o(1)]. Either constant densities, or AD (p_j = 1), or a global selection argument is
   308	needed.
   309	
   310	**Union-bound route to AD.** To get "every target is good" from field moments one needs Pr_A[τ dead] ≤ e^{−b₁Q} per
   311	target. Chebyshev/Paley–Zygmund give only Pr[dead] ≤ Var/E² ≥ Θ(1) here, because descendability has Θ(1) relative
   312	variance across fields (local factors: root counts of f mod small primes). E1 sees this directly: per-field
   313	uniform rates range from 0.11 to 0.61 (§9). Higher moments of order k ≤ d/2 would need joint asymptotics of
   314	2k-point smoothness with relative error e^{−Ω(kE)}, far beyond any smooth-number estimate.
   315	
   316	## 7. What ERH does buy
   317	
   318	**Lemma X_ERH [proved from GRH-Chebotarev].** Assume GRH for the Dedekind zeta functions of the fields
   319	L_h = K(√h), h ∈ H ∖ {1} (lv6 §3.2–3.3 notation; [L_h : Q] = 2d, |Δ_{L_h}| ≤ Δ̄ with log Δ̄ = O(d·t)). Sample
   320	first-degree primes 𝔭 = (p, s) of K with N𝔭 ∈ (x/2, x] and p ∤ D as in lv6 (rejecting the ≤ log₂|D| primes p | D),
   321	with x the least integer such that √x ≥ C₀(d + log Δ̄)²(log Δ̄)² and log x ≥ b₁Q + 1. Then for every h ≠ 1, Pr_𝔭[χ_𝔭(h) = −1] ≥ 1/(5d), and with
   322	t_χ := ⌈5d(ln|H| + 2 ln t)⌉ characters the joint kernel meets H only in {1} with probability ≥ 1 − t^{−2}. No
   323	exceptional class (lv6 Lemma E) occurs.
   324	
   325	*Proof.* Lagarias–Odlyzko 1977, Thm 1.1 (GRH version): for L/K Galois with group G and class C,
   326	|π_C(x) − (|C|/|G|)Li(x)| ≤ c₁((|C|/|G|)x^{1/2}log(d_L x^{n_L}) + log d_L), c₁ absolute. Apply to L_h/K, G = C₂,
   327	C = {σ}: the number of primes of K with Frobenius σ and norm in (x/2, x] is
   328	≥ ½(Li(x) − Li(x/2)) − 2c₁(√x(log Δ̄ + 2d log x) + log Δ̄) ≥ x/(5 log x) for the stated x. Subtract the ≤ d√x primes
   329	of degree ≥ 2 and the ≤ log₂Δ̄ ramified ones. Apply the same theorem to K/K (G trivial; GRH for ζ_K) for an upper
   330	bound: first-degree primes with norm in (x/2, x] number ≤ d(π(x) − π(x/2)) ≤ 1.1dx/log x. The ratio is ≥ 1/(5d).
   331	Independence and a union bound over |H| classes give the kernel statement; under GRH no h has an exceptional real
   332	zero, so H₀ = {1}. The condition log x ≥ b₁Q + 1 makes every character prime larger than every prime > B that divides an
   333	atom of a multi-level row (such primes are targets, of norm ≤ e^{b₁Q}), and the rejection of p | D keeps character
   334	primes away from D; primes ≤ B are below x. Sampling and evaluation cost poly(log x) = poly(t). ∎
   335	
   336	The x-threshold is polylogarithmic in N for d ≤ t, so LV6's character step works at any growing degree under ERH.
   337	**Correction to recurse §3.5:** without ERH, the Stark threshold ν₁ ≍ 1/((2d)!log Δ̄) forces log x ≳ (2d)!·log Δ̄,
   338	i.e. characters of (2d)!-bit size; at α = 1/3 with d = δD′ this is e^{Θ(δ)E}·poly, an L[1/3, Θ(δ)] factor (still
   339	finite), not L^{o(1)} as recurse §3.5 says (that statement is right for α > 1/3). LV's own §6 (unconditional,
   340	Stark–Brauer–Siegel with an exceptional field set) is the unconditional alternative; we did not transfer it.
   341	
   342	**Can ERH be weakened or averaged?** For Lemma X_ERH: yes in principle (LV §6, unconditional). Family-averaged
   343	zero-free regions (Pierce–Turnage-Butterbaugh–Wood, Invent. Math. 2020; Lemke Oliver–Thorner–Zaman 2024) are for
   344	families of *fixed* degree and fixed Galois group; nothing is known for growing-degree families such as ours. For
   345	nested descent ERH is not the missing input at all (§§3–5), so weakening it changes nothing there. For dBPW the
   346	Hecke ERH drives the random walk; averaging over our random fields would need the spectral gap for growing-degree
   347	Arakelov groups on average, which is open, and in any case Theorem AF caps the outcome at L[1/2].
   348	
   349	**The adaptive one-sided lemma (codex nested_1 §2) [proved under ERH, from Thm 9.5].** If the target ideal and the
   350	family 𝒮 are arbitrary functions of the history and δ_𝒮[r^n] ≥ η for every history, then with fresh coins and
   351	ε ≤ η/6 each attempt succeeds with probability ≥ η/6 conditionally on the history. This genuinely handles endogenous
   352	*one-sided* children; its price is the Theorem-AF norm scale.
   353	
   354	## 8. Theorem NR: ERH + AD ⇒ L[1/3]
   355	
   356	Parameters: constants δ, λ ∈ (0, 1), b, b₁, s > 0 with τ_F := δb₁/2 < 1. d := the least odd integer ≥ δD′;
   357	B := ⌊e^{bE}⌋. Field law 𝔉: any Las Vegas-samplable law of fields A = (f, g₀, g₁) with f irreducible of degree d
   358	(with a certificate), F(g₀, g₁) ≡ 0 (mod N), gcd(g₀, g₁) = 1, and log‖f‖_∞, log max(g₀, g₁) ≤ κQ for a constant
   359	κ (e.g. design (a‴) with M = ⌊N^{1/(d+1)}⌋, w ≈ M, H ≈ M). D_A := N·g₁·f_d·disc(f).
   360	
   361	*Targets* 𝒯(A): rational primes q and degree-one prime ideals (q, r) (r a simple root of f mod q) with
   362	B < q ≤ e^{b₁Q} and q ∤ D_A. *Box* of a target of norm q = e^z: the points P = (a, b) of its lattice with
   363	gcd(a, b) = 1, 0 < b ≤ V, |a| ≤ V, V := ⌊√q·e^{sE}⌋, G(P)F(P) ≠ 0. *Good point:* every prime p dividing the
   364	target's two cofactors (G(P)/q or G(P); F(P)/q or F(P)) satisfies p ≤ B, or p ≤ e^{λz} and p ∤ D_A.
   365	
   366	**Hypothesis AD(δ, λ, b, b₁, s; 𝔉).** For all large N, Pr_{A∼𝔉}[every τ ∈ 𝒯(A) has a good point] ≥ 1/2.
   367	
   368	**Hypothesis LV6′.** lv6's Lemmas C, H, SQ and IR (good primes, residual square-class group, square root in Z[β],
   369	irreducibility certificate) hold at degree d ≍ D′ with bounds polynomial in d and t. (UNVERIFIED bookkeeping; the
   370	only growing-degree gap identified in recurse §3.5 was Lemma X, which Lemma X_ERH replaces.)
   371	
   372	**Theorem NR [proved conditional on ERH, AD and LV6′].** There is a Las Vegas algorithm that outputs the complete
   373	factorisation of every N in expected time L_N[1/3, c + o(1)] for every
   374	c > c_NR := max(2b, (b + 1/b₁)/(1 − 2^{−1/3}), (b + 2s)/(1 − τ_F^{1/3})).
   375	
   376	*Algorithm NEST* (run inside the recurse wrapper: preprocessing, trial division to B, perfect powers, AKS, and
   377	alternation with trial division instead of an N₀ branch, recurse §1.1 item 3 / auditRA):
   378	1. Sample A ∼ 𝔉 (certificates; gcd checks of g₁, f_d, disc f, content with N; a nontrivial gcd is a factor exit).
   379	2. Rows. Repeat: r uniform in [1, N−1] (gcd check), R := r², Gauss-reduce to (u₀, v₀); completely factor |u₀|, |v₀|
   380	   by recursive calls with fresh coins; accept iff every prime factor p satisfies p ≤ B or (p ≤ e^{b₁Q}, p ∤ D_A).
   381	   For each accepted row, run DESC on every prime factor > B; if any DESC returns FAIL, discard A and all rows and go
   382	   to step 1.
   383	   DESC(τ): scan the box of τ in a fixed order; for each P, gcd-check G(P), F(P) with N, completely factor both by
   384	   recursive calls; at the first good point, run DESC on every child (prime > B of the cofactors; algebraic children
   385	   are (p, a·b^{−1} mod p)) and return the tree; if the box has no good point return FAIL.
   386	3. When M_rows := #columns + 12 rows are collected, run LV6 with Lemma X_ERH (columns: sign, v_p mod 2 for p ≤ B,
   387	   parity column, (p, s) for p ≤ B, p ∤ D, t_χ characters), the rows being given by Lemma RW below; compute
   388	   X = ∏_{i∈S} r_i, Y, and gcd(X − Y, N). On a trivial gcd discard the rows and collect fresh ones (same field).
   389	
   390	**Lemma RW (row identity for multi-level trees) [proved].** Let t := f_d, β := tα, z_P := ta − bβ ∈ Z[β], so
   391	ψ(z_P) ≡ t·G(P)/g₁ (mod N) (thmA §3). Give each tree node v an exponent ε_v ∈ Z: a root prime ℓ gets +v_ℓ(u₀) or
   392	−v_ℓ(v₀); a child of v gets −ε_v times its valuation in v's *rational value* (n_v := |G(P_v)|/q for a rational
   393	target, |G(P_v)| for an algebraic one) or in v's *element* z_{P_v} (algebraic children). Then
   394	  r² ≡ q_row·ψ(Z_row) (mod N),  Z_row := ∏_v z_{P_v}^{ε_v},
   395	with q_row ∈ Q^× supported on −1, g₁, t and primes ≤ B, and the ideal of Z_row supported on first-degree primes of
   396	norm ≤ B and on primes dividing D. *Proof.* Induction on the number of expanded nodes, with the invariant
   397	"r² ≡ (product of open targets and closed factors) mod N". Expanding a rational target q with exponent ε uses
   398	q = σn_v^{−1}G(P) ≡ σg₁t^{−1}ψ(z_P)/n_v, which removes q^ε, adds z_P^ε to Z and n_v^{−ε} to the rational side; the
   399	algebraic primes of z_P^ε become open with exponents ε·v_𝔭(z_P), and the rational primes of n_v^{−ε} become open.
   400	Expanding an algebraic target 𝔮 with exponent ε_w multiplies Z by z_{P′}^{ε_w} and the rational side by
   401	(tG(P′)/g₁)^{−ε_w}; since P′ is good, v_𝔮(z_{P′}) = 1 (otherwise q would divide the cofactor, and q > e^{λz}), so
   402	the ε_w = −ε_parent·v_𝔮(z_P) exactly cancels 𝔮 in the ideal of Z. A good point's children do not divide D, so all
   403	non-factor-base primes are opened and later closed; the tree is finite (below), so at the end only factor-base and
   404	D-primes remain. ∎ The exponents satisfy |ε_v| ≤ t^{depth} = e^{O((log t)²)}. LV6 applies with Z_i replaced by
   405	Z_i⁺Z_i⁻ (Z_i = Z_i⁺/Z_i⁻ with Z_i^± ∈ Z[β]), which differs by a square; characters are unaffected because
   406	character primes do not divide atoms (Lemma X_ERH).
   407	
   408	**Lemma TS (tree size) [proved].** In any field, a DESC tree has depth ≤ ⌈log(b₁Q/(bE))/log(1/λ)⌉ + 1 = O(log t)
   409	(children of a target e^z are ≤ e^{λz}, and targets are > B) and each node has ≤ 2·(log|F(P)| + log|G(P)|)/log B
   410	= O(t/E) children, so ≤ e^{O((log t)²)} = e^{o(E)} nodes. The box has ≤ 5e^{2sE} points. (codex (30).)
   411	
   412	**Lemma T_multi (transcript invariance for adaptive multi-level trees) [proved].** Every exposed quantity of NEST
   413	(field, R_i, (u₀, v₀), canonical factorisations returned by recursive calls, all box scans, all failed and
   414	successful DESC calls and their order, field restarts, columns, characters, kernel vectors, Hensel prime, Y) is a
   415	function of (R_i) and coins independent of the hidden roots: DESC is a deterministic function of (A, target) given
   416	the canonical factorisations, and recursive calls use fresh coins (recurse Lemmas OS, T_rec). Hence, as in Theorem
